id: recipe.webhook-receiver
kind: recipe
title: Webhook receiver
aliases: webhook endpoint, incoming webhooks
question: how would you design an endpoint that receives third-party webhooks?
tags: architecture, api, reliability
properties:
  - fast acknowledgement (third parties time out in seconds)
  - signature verification
  - duplicate-safe (senders retry)
  - replayable history for debugging
invariant: Acknowledge within the sender's timeout, verify authenticity before acting, and process asynchronously with deduplication -- senders retry, so delivery is at-least-once.
answer_30s: Verify the signature, persist the raw event with its provider id, return 2xx immediately, then process asynchronously with deduplication on that id.
flow: |
  Provider -> Ingress/API (verify signature, persist raw event)
     |
  immediate 2xx (within the provider's timeout)
     |
  queue -> worker (dedupe on provider event id, apply effect)
     |
  store: raw events + processed status (replayable)
compose:
  - design.idempotency => the same webhook will arrive more than once
  - design.exactly-once => delivery is at-least-once; the effect must be single
  - design.producer-consumer => acknowledgement and processing are decoupled
  - design.bounded-queue => a slow downstream must not make the endpoint time out
  - design.state-machine => received/verified/processed/failed are explicit states
  - design.retry-backoff => our own downstream retries are bounded and jittered
  - design.observability => signature failures and queue depth are the two signals that matter
  - design.health-checking => readiness must not depend on the downstream that processes asynchronously
  - k8s.service => stable endpoint for the provider
  - rust.axum => handler verifies and enqueues, nothing slow in the request path
  - rust.tokio-mpsc => bounded hand-off to the processing side
failure_modes:
  - processing synchronously, so the provider times out and retries (duplicating the effect)
  - no signature verification, so anyone can inject events
  - no deduplication, so provider retries double-apply
  - 5xx returned for a downstream problem, so the provider retries for hours (return 2xx and record it)
follow_ups:
  - the provider sends a duplicate after a week -- what happens?
related:
  - design.idempotency
  - design.exactly-once
  - recipe.async-job-processing
sources:
  - https://stripe.com/docs/webhooks/best-practices
